      <h2 id="olm-managing-custom-catalogs-fb" class="group scroll-mt-20">File-based catalogs<a href="#olm-managing-custom-catalogs-fb" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p><em>File-based catalogs</em> are the latest iteration of the catalog format in Operator Lifecycle Manager (OLM). It is a plain text-based (JSON or YAML) and declarative config evolution of the earlier SQLite database format, and it is fully backwards compatible.</p>
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>As of OpenShift Container Platform 4.11, the default Red Hat-provided Operator catalog releases in the file-based catalog format. The default Red Hat-provided Operator catalogs for OpenShift Container Platform 4.6 through 4.10 released in the deprecated SQLite database format.</p>
          <p>The <code>opm</code> subcommands, flags, and functionality related to the SQLite database format are also deprecated and will be removed in a future release. The features are still supported and must be used for catalogs that use the deprecated SQLite database format.</p>
          <p>Many of the <code>opm</code> subcommands and flags for working with the SQLite database format, such as <code>opm index prune</code>, do not work with the file-based catalog format. For more information about working with file-based catalogs, see "Operator Framework packaging format" and "Mirroring images for a disconnected installation using the oc-mirror plugin".</p>
